<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Brunswick</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1918 CE · capital Brunswick</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North German duchy of the House of Welf, ruled by the 'Black Duke' and later the subject of a long succession dispute.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</a> had been absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> in 1807. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Duke Frederick William</a>, the Black Duke, who had fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-brunswickers">Black Brunswickers</a>, recovered his lands in 1813, and in 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> recognised them as the sovereign Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. He was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-quatre-bras">Quatre Bras</a> that June.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-brunswick">Charles II</a> was driven out by a rising in 1830 and replaced by his brother William, who granted a constitution in 1832. The duchy was Lutheran and had industry around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> beet and canning. When William died in 1884, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> blocked the Hanoverian heir, and regents ruled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-duke-of-brunswick">Ernest Augustus</a> married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser's</a> daughter <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Victoria</a> Louise in 1913 and became duke.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-duke-of-brunswick">Ernest Augustus</a> abdicated in November 1918, and the duchy became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-brunswick">Free State of Brunswick</a>. In 1946 its territory was merged into the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, though the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Braunschweig</a> keeps the ducal palace, rebuilt in 2007.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-brunswick.jpg" alt="Duchy of Brunswick" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Siebrand · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Herzogtum_Braunschweig_(1814-1830).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Brunswick</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick William, William, Ernest Augustus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Herzogtum Braunschweig</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1866&amp;focus=duchy-of-brunswick" aria-label="Duchy of Brunswick on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4917 373 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Brunswick lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5237 629L5237 632M5237 629L5199 691M5237 629L5300 622M5237 629L5237 629"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5237 632h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0M5237 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5237 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5237 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Brunswick. <a href="/atlas?year=1866&amp;focus=duchy-of-brunswick">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
